## REASONING

The user's request is, at its core, about **lateral subsurface transport**: hillslope-to-stream travel times, lateral flow convergence along topographic flowpaths, and groundwater exchange *between neighboring hillslope positions*. Every one of these three mechanisms is a lateral, column-to-column process.

The framework's PRIMARY model, ELM, is explicitly SINGLE-COLUMN with NO LATERAL TRANSPORT — columns are independent 1-D points with no routing, no lateral/regional groundwater flow, and no column-to-column exchange. This is not a peripheral limitation; it is precisely the mechanism the question asks about. There is no lever (soil, forcing period, spin-up, elevation-via-NLDAS) that lets a stack of independent 1-D columns represent flow *between* columns or compute a travel time *along a flowpath*.

Therefore the design cannot be presented as a "site" spatial-sampling campaign that answers the question, because placing N columns along a hillslope transect would still give N independent 1-D water balances — the convergence and exchange terms (the entire point) would be identically zero by construction. Reporting travel times from such a setup would be fabricating a mechanism the runs don't contain.

What the stack CAN contribute is a partial, boundary-condition role: ELM columns placed along a topographic sequence (ridge → midslope → valley/riparian) can produce the *vertical* recharge/drainage flux (QDRAI) and soil-moisture states that would FEED a lateral flow model. Genuinely answering travel times and inter-hillslope exchange requires a lateral solver — either the proposed (unbuilt) ELM→PFLOTRAN weak coupling driving a hillslope-resolving PFLOTRAN mesh, or a hillslope/routing model. Both are Tier-3 gaps.

So: verdict **infeasible** as posed. I document the ELM contribution honestly as a recharge-BC generator, list the lateral-transport machinery under requires_capabilities, and note there are no in-domain observations to validate against regardless. N is kept minimal (a single ridge-to-valley toposequence) because its only defensible use is generating BC fluxes, not resolving the requested lateral physics.

